{"gid":"2026_03_ATL_GB","week":3,"away":"ATL","home":"GB","away_col":"#FF525B","home_col":"#FFD84D","spread":7.5,"total":46.5,"fav":"GB","hpts":27,"apts":20,"kickoff":"Thu Sep 24 \u00b7 20:15","roof":"Outdoors","report_html":"<div class=\"report\"><p><strong>Ground mismatch:</strong> GB are run-first (36% pass when leading) into a ATL front that bleeds <strong>124 rush yds/game</strong> and 0.32 RB1 rushing scores. <strong>MarShawn Lloyd</strong> is a centerpiece \u2014 and the game script only feeds him.</p><p>The <strong>GB</strong> host the <strong>ATL</strong> as favorites of <strong>7.5</strong>, with the total set at <strong>46.5</strong> \u2014 a market that implies a <strong>GB 27\u201320</strong> scoreline. Running the game forward from that baseline, we expect a contest that spends roughly <strong>39% of its snaps with GB in front</strong>, <strong>35% within one score</strong>, and <strong>26% with GB chasing</strong>. That distribution is the engine of everything below: game state is what turns a team's tendencies into a specific player's workload, so before we get to individuals it's worth being explicit about <em>how</em> this game is likely to be played.</p><p>This projects as a game <strong>GB should control</strong>. A favorite of 8 sits in front for the majority of the second half in most simulations, which pulls the script toward the run and clock management for <strong>GB</strong> and toward volume passing and tempo for <strong>ATL</strong>, who will be throwing to keep pace. Expect <strong>ATL</strong> to run more total plays than GB \u2014 trailing teams go no-huddle (league no-huddle rate climbs from ~8% in neutral game states to over 20% when down two scores), and that extra volume is a tailwind for ATL's pass-catchers even in a losing effort.</p><h3>The Key Matchups</h3><p>Tough sledding for <strong>Bijan Robinson</strong> \u2014 GB are stingy against the run (110 yds/game, well below the ~120 norm); lean under on his rushing line.</p><p><strong>Christian Watson</strong> faces a tough cover \u2014 ATL hold WR1s to just 52 yds/game (vs ~58); a rec-yards under lean.</p><p><strong>Drake London</strong> faces a tough cover \u2014 GB hold WR1s to just 48 yds/game (vs ~58); a rec-yards under lean.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>Bijan Robinson</strong> is a bell-cow (65% of carries) meeting a defense that allows 0.50 RB rushing TDs/game \u2014 strong anytime-TD equity.</p><p><strong>Christian Watson</strong> carries TD equity \u2014 ATL surrender 0.47 WR1 receiving scores a game.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p><strong>Script leans GB.</strong> As a 8-point favorite they project to lead much of the game, tilting toward the run late \u2014 <strong>MarShawn Lloyd</strong>'s carry ceiling rises in the blowout branches. Meanwhile ATL throw to keep pace, a volume tailwind for <strong>Drake London</strong>.</p><p>A middling 46 total \u2014 no strong lean either way; the value is in the individual matchups above, not the game environment.</p><h3>GB \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>GB are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>36%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>This is the profile that most rewards a lead back. As a 8-point favorite projected to lead for 39% of snaps, <strong>GB</strong> will be running to close the game, and their run-first identity compounds it. <strong>MarShawn Lloyd</strong> is the beneficiary \u2014 we project <strong>19 carries for 69 yards</strong>, and his ceiling is where the value sits: in the simulations where this becomes a two-score game, his volume spikes as GB salt it away.</p><p>The flip side: <strong>Christian Watson</strong> (9 targets, 81 yards) has his ceiling <em>capped</em> by the same script that helps the run game \u2014 when GB lead and run, there simply aren't as many pass attempts to go around. He's a safer floor than ceiling play in this spot.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Tucker Kraft</strong> (TE, 8 targets, 66 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>ATL \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>ATL are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>33%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 ATL project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>Bijan Robinson</strong>'s rushing projection (21 carries, 101 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Drake London</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, ATL funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>10 targets for 71 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Kyle Pitts</strong> (TE, 8 targets, 57 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 8-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</st